---
title: "Basic tutorial 8: Check tissues into a freezer box"
authors:
  - Heru Handika
lastUpdated: 2026-11-20
sidebar:
  label: "8. Check tissues into a box"
  order: 8
---

After fieldwork, tissue tubes go into freezer boxes at the museum. The paper
catalog records which specimens have a tissue, but not where each tube ends up.
Specimen curation writes each tube's museum code and storage location to its
part record as you place it, so the box and the database stay in sync.

In this tutorial, you will check the six liver tubes from Mt. Tujuh into one
box: five by tissue ID and one by field ID.

## 1. Set up the box

{% steps %}

1. On the `Dashboard`, select `Curation` in the tool dock.
2. Open `Setup`. Under `Find tubes`, set `Tissue type` to `Liver` and
   `Preservation` to `ETOH`.
3. Under `Update field`, choose `Museum permanent` and enter the museum code as
   `Value`. This tutorial uses `EXM`.
4. Under `Box`, set the box to match the one on your bench. `Box label` and
   `Box number` name the box in each tube's `Storage location`. Use the label
   for a freezer or rack prefix, such as `F2 Box`. You can also set
   `Box size` and `Fill order`. This tutorial keeps the defaults: `Box` and
   `1`, which give `Box 1`, with 81 cells filled in rows from left to right.

{% /steps %}

![NAHPU Specimen curation on an iPad with an empty Box 1 and Setup set to find liver tubes in ETOH and write Museum permanent EXM](../../../../../assets/images/app-screenshots/tutorials/basic/curation-setup.webp)

## 2. Find tubes by tissue ID

Five tubes carry the `Z` tissue numbers you entered on the `Liver` parts in
Tutorial 5.

{% steps %}

1. Select `Scan` above the scan field.
2. Enter `Z26230` and press Enter. NAHPU reports the tube and its cell:
   `Z26230 → Box 1: A1 · Museum permanent = EXM`.
3. Place the tube in that cell.
4. Repeat for `Z26231`, `Z26247`, `Z26250`, and `Z26317`.

{% /steps %}

The five tubes fill `A1` to `A5`. Each cell shows the last characters of its
tissue ID.

![NAHPU Specimen curation on an iPad with tubes 6230, 6231, 6247, 6250, and 6317 in cells A1 to A5 of Box 1, and Z26317 placed in Box 1: A5](../../../../../assets/images/app-screenshots/tutorials/basic/curation-tissue-id.webp)

## 3. Find a tube without a tissue number by field ID

One tube is left: `HH529`'s liver. Young Heru left its label blank in 2014,
so it has no tissue number to enter. Without a tissue number, you can still find
the tube by its field ID. Turn on `Search by field ID` and enter `HH529`.
Because `Find tubes` is limited to liver in ETOH, the field ID matches only that
tube, and NAHPU places it in the next cell:
`HH529 · Liver · ETOH → Box 1: A6 · Museum permanent = EXM`.

![NAHPU Specimen curation on an iPad with Search by field ID turned on and HH529's liver in ETOH placed in Box 1: A6](../../../../../assets/images/app-screenshots/tutorials/basic/curation-field-id.webp)

{% aside type="tip" title="Write the field number on tissue tubes" %}
It is also good practice to write the field number on tissue tubes, next to the
tissue number. If a tissue number is missing or unreadable, the field number
still leads you to the right tube.
{% /aside %}

## 4. Keep the log

Open `Log` to see the six checked tubes and their cells. Select `Export log` to
save the session as CSV, TSV, or Excel, and keep it with the box inventory as a
record of every placement.

![NAHPU Specimen curation Log on an iPad listing six checked tubes, from Z26230 in Box 1: A1 to HH529 Liver ETOH in Box 1: A6, with no skipped tubes](../../../../../assets/images/app-screenshots/tutorials/basic/curation-log.webp)

## 5. Check the Data table

The museum codes and storage locations are now saved on each liver part. To
check them all at once, open `Data table` under `Tools` on the dashboard,
choose `Specimen parts`, and search for `Liver`. Under the columns button, turn
on `museumPermanent` and `storageLocation`. Every liver tube now reads `EXM`
and has its cell, from `Box 1: A1` to `Box 1: A6`, including `HH529`'s, which
has no tissue ID:

![NAHPU Data table on an iPad showing the six liver parts with tissue ID, type, treatment, field number, museumPermanent EXM, and storage locations Box 1: A1 to Box 1: A6; the row for field number 529 has no tissue ID](../../../../../assets/images/app-screenshots/tutorials/basic/data-table-curated-parts.webp)

Later, you can export these records to match the data input of your collection
management system for the permanent database.

## What you gained over paper

- Each tube's location is written to its record as you place it, not copied
  from a box sheet later.
- A missing tissue number does not stop the check; the field ID finds the tube.
- The session log keeps every placement for review.

